                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e6b5bbe8-34ed-44eb-aad7-dc5cfaabb86b",
                        "source_entry_id": "e6e1ed55-90cc-306a-abc0-2508f51603bc",
                        "source_sense_id": "498ece70-3650-35a3-b483-29c76a23c794",
                        "target_lang_word": "bludgeon",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bludgeon",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20f226ae-ced5-3856-b547-b8680faac42e",
                                        "entry_id": "531f9f58-1c57-3ed1-ba82-eb54fa334497",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A short, heavy club, often of wood, which is thicker or loaded at one end."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "20c7c67b-47eb-4094-b0cc-fb8161555ef7",
                                                "sense_id": "20f226ae-ced5-3856-b547-b8680faac42e",
                                                "text": "We smashed the radio with a steel bludgeon."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "bludgeon",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d7b945a3-6adb-36d7-9674-58b566749215",
                                        "entry_id": "9bb67499-e645-3c91-bc54-18b7272c6cd7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To strike or hit with something hard, usually on the head; to club."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c0e65eb3-52bf-4720-b843-7b324bb78b32",
                                                "sense_id": "d7b945a3-6adb-36d7-9674-58b566749215",
                                                "text": "The apprehended rioter was bludgeoned to death."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "19b7861d-5b75-49fa-94ab-7761c92d1a67",
                                                "sense_id": "d7b945a3-6adb-36d7-9674-58b566749215",
                                                "text": "They didn't get shot to death in hold-ups, strangled to death in rapes, stabbed to death in saloons, bludgeoned to death with axes by parents or children or die summarily by some other act of God."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8ee8e254-0811-458c-a97c-c45121d8473a",
                                                "sense_id": "d7b945a3-6adb-36d7-9674-58b566749215",
                                                "text": "Thomas Briggs, a senior bank clerk in the City, lived in Hackney, and on 9 July 1864 he was returning there by train from Fenchurch Street after a Saturday spent at work when he was bludgeoned to death in a first-class carriage, probably by a young German tailor named Franz Müller. (Let's hope it was Müller, because he was hanged for it.) And so Briggs was an all-round pioneer: an early commuter and the very first victim of a railway murder."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8fe243e6-6b7f-395c-a15d-524a5cc702e2",
                                        "entry_id": "9bb67499-e645-3c91-bc54-18b7272c6cd7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To coerce someone, as if with a bludgeon."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c05ee35f-a6bd-4ce1-94d0-14a4d988ecdb",
                                                "sense_id": "8fe243e6-6b7f-395c-a15d-524a5cc702e2",
                                                "text": "Their favorite method was bludgeoning us with the same old arguments in favor of their opinions."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db731b25-829c-4919-b6ee-1952fba95233",
                                                "sense_id": "8fe243e6-6b7f-395c-a15d-524a5cc702e2",
                                                "text": "Gianna Parasini: You've never worked in the corporate world, have you, Commander? You can't bludgeon through bureaucracy.\nShepard: I can bludgeon pretty hard."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "57eee14d-38c1-4029-a15e-3a6fab23f33e",
                        "source_entry_id": "e6e1ed55-90cc-306a-abc0-2508f51603bc",
                        "source_sense_id": "498ece70-3650-35a3-b483-29c76a23c794",
                        "target_lang_word": "cudgel",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cudgel",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7dbebcf2-4840-3e71-8476-0f2c1ea0ac04",
                                        "entry_id": "d4130f5d-c555-3c79-b252-3b191456f107",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A short heavy club with a rounded head used as a weapon."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75d2cceb-1259-40b0-b154-ae7e67a17c14",
                                                "sense_id": "7dbebcf2-4840-3e71-8476-0f2c1ea0ac04",
                                                "text": "The guard hefted his cudgel menacingly and looked at the inmates."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2a550dc2-60da-44a3-bfcb-ab289887f0a3",
                                                "sense_id": "7dbebcf2-4840-3e71-8476-0f2c1ea0ac04",
                                                "text": "Then they had bouts of wrestling and of cudgel play, so that every day they gained in skill and strength."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a376fcc5-ac26-4559-b022-7c0f9311af19",
                                                "sense_id": "7dbebcf2-4840-3e71-8476-0f2c1ea0ac04",
                                                "text": "So when he aroſe, he getteth him a grievous Crab-tree cudgel, and goes down into the Dungeon to them; and there firſt falls to Rateing of them, as if they were dogs: […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5abfebb6-87b8-3dbf-a461-ae6039f2f6ab",
                                        "entry_id": "d4130f5d-c555-3c79-b252-3b191456f107",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Anything that can be used as a threat to force one's will on another."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06947fe6-7ed2-4f9c-9fbe-109d9a0081d1",
                                                "sense_id": "5abfebb6-87b8-3dbf-a461-ae6039f2f6ab",
                                                "text": "As above said, legibility depends also much on the design of the letter; and again I take up the cudgels against compressed type, and that especially in Roman letters: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "894ec7be-9a47-4b5b-b0fb-7bb5c4cc1112",
                                                "sense_id": "5abfebb6-87b8-3dbf-a461-ae6039f2f6ab",
                                                "text": "Mrs. Clinton’s Senate tenure, however, also demonstrated the risks of overcompensation: Not wanting to give Republicans fodder to portray her as soft on defense, she authorized President Bush to use force in Iraq and handed Mr. Obama a political cudgel to use against her."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "11808159-47ba-4edb-b51b-21e13a58ce2c",
                                                "sense_id": "5abfebb6-87b8-3dbf-a461-ae6039f2f6ab",
                                                "text": "[Minnesota Senator Steve] Daines isn’t the only example of right-wing politicians who wish to wield anti-Semitism as a convenient cudgel against their political enemies, with scant if any evidence. But Montana’s vanishingly small Jewish population makes it particularly clear that this strategy has little to do with flesh-and-blood Jews at all."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ca7b0afc-83d3-4c0f-86da-c66a75551b21",
                                                "sense_id": "5abfebb6-87b8-3dbf-a461-ae6039f2f6ab",
                                                "text": "Mr. Cruz has been able to use his pseudo-intellectualism and his Ivy League pedigree as a cudgel."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "cudgel",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "79f3a720-59e1-3f3f-989d-24fdbf27c634",
                                        "entry_id": "bd3c89a1-c9b0-3731-8d56-ff572bc4cba2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To strike with a cudgel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3db1219f-4390-4137-97b2-bb6751f2d8f1",
                                                "sense_id": "79f3a720-59e1-3f3f-989d-24fdbf27c634",
                                                "text": "The officer was violently cudgeled down in the midst of the rioters."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8635d3c0-3b38-4f9c-8890-fd5edec7b3a8",
                                                "sense_id": "79f3a720-59e1-3f3f-989d-24fdbf27c634",
                                                "text": "I would cudgel him like a dog if he would say so."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bb9feea9-4c46-4768-a014-0e09e4243ad3",
                                                "sense_id": "79f3a720-59e1-3f3f-989d-24fdbf27c634",
                                                "text": "Poets like Cudgel'd Bullys, never do / At firſt, or ſecond blow, ſubmit to you; / But will provoke you ſtill and ne're have done, / Till you are weary ſirst, with laying on: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cf2eeb9e-b029-44ea-94e7-1f05cdfed274",
                                                "sense_id": "79f3a720-59e1-3f3f-989d-24fdbf27c634",
                                                "text": "Aboard the barge and so off the trail, the blessing lost its puissance and the barge-tender, who coveted Guyal's rich accoutrements, sought to cudgel him with a knoblolly."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8e82a611-d3d1-37cd-8033-b22cb3882ae8",
                                        "entry_id": "bd3c89a1-c9b0-3731-8d56-ff572bc4cba2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To exercise (one's wits or brains) in an effort to force a memory or solution; to rack (one's mind)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d59aa678-5637-4e16-b1d0-b7582a721863",
                                                "sense_id": "8e82a611-d3d1-37cd-8033-b22cb3882ae8",
                                                "text": "“Most remarkable,” murmured Tarzan, cudgeling his brain for some pretext upon which to turn the subject."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "17c19ea0-24ee-4135-a2a8-861add54318f",
                        "source_entry_id": "e6e1ed55-90cc-306a-abc0-2508f51603bc",
